#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f9894f-9b1a-5900-9728-e89ee154ca0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{254\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436636} {:answer/id #uuid "68aa1ac1-e8a7-508d-88c0-b565a4e94a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{508\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436637} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccd92ca-3dd3-51a1-b84b-9d383e7fe955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{255\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436638} {:answer/id #uuid "91f7c777-0592-5298-8f5a-8cd55fbdab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{510\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436639} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8af1eb-e88d-5f7f-b4bb-c743c4774a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68aa1ac1-e8a7-508d-88c0-b565a4e94a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{508\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436637} :answer-field/id #uuid "77ba2466-990e-5f7d-96e7-58f6c3675c84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50c86907-f8d2-5518-873d-192b1784708d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161119" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16e4559960b923535cadecb1e623592c07fa11de5929e722cffb85679195a66.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}^{3/4}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}({r}^{2})^{3/4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{3/2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{2}{7}{r}^{7/2}]}_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(128-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{508\\pi }{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7bcdd2-a4aa-5f5a-8f98-2422d1091a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352783} {:answer/id #uuid "ac919fed-1071-5ed1-ac5f-813f92578d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353032} {:answer/id #uuid "afa4880d-8495-5e0c-a30f-f4a9c595deec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353064} {:answer/id #uuid "b0156a3c-6e2e-5c24-8878-51857602147d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353073} {:answer/id #uuid "d18eeca6-da7c-589c-a965-b05476689840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa4880d-8495-5e0c-a30f-f4a9c595deec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353064} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a5bb53-0890-5adf-a609-e4bea366a209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9578f94f-5956-53e9-ae71-c25041b507cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83999" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0d72be437a462f5eea781e83cc2a95e956e11ddc3295ad3b02a4a57867d9271.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{3},-1).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/23/2386c1dcb1cf71231f2bb508dc1dd85dadef15b806eaf7f9a9ad0d3e4f3da1ca.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6} \\\\  & =[(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(0-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8f91d3-c985-5ff9-a7d5-30ebbe848335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352276} {:answer/id #uuid "616be36f-20a7-5594-9222-5abdb16a236b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352438} {:answer/id #uuid "79385752-aec6-56af-a9f5-88f94e3cb797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352624} {:answer/id #uuid "9294ec35-9104-50c2-abc4-83106e810f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352836} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ba7cbd-8617-5aa0-9cf3-e77cd9c04b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79385752-aec6-56af-a9f5-88f94e3cb797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352624} :answer-field/id #uuid "379a280a-d1e2-57b2-b66e-8dfa34c9f758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5f310f9-e63f-56fe-abce-21ad9b7c4099" :question/math-academy-id "q-85065" :question/problem "![](images/15/15e58073c87bd249473950680a49e8fc4cb4be94277a7001c0ab9f96ab3a46df.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-3,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/92/928c6dc79f44a2185ccf217c0cf9210630b6554a698f805ef2c77500f9ddc76b.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04830521-3355-5cb5-969c-1650b3f830fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351611} {:answer/id #uuid "100cff3b-1934-5849-b10d-7ff26075848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351704} {:answer/id #uuid "148a4d56-41a6-57ea-804c-a991e5584c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351745} {:answer/id #uuid "82ffbdc0-51d3-5215-90bd-435914ccdf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} {:answer/id #uuid "f86b2a50-5585-5203-b724-06d25c9c5e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ffbdc0-51d3-5215-90bd-435914ccdf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9760e9-c40f-5ec5-866a-aa3fc636ddf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a399d3dc-47f2-5985-b430-d143a3ff87d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85182" :question/problem "![](images/51/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/bf/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013b7b16-b535-5412-8ea8-edd8fe941911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351575} {:answer/id #uuid "0a07701f-e9ed-5bcd-8b82-07920ae0db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351654} {:answer/id #uuid "7747850c-b635-58ff-becc-cebb5b7aea70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352612} {:answer/id #uuid "9051911f-0beb-5472-ab3e-0a639b001b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352817} {:answer/id #uuid "c4342d60-22f3-5739-b274-49639ed612bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9051911f-0beb-5472-ab3e-0a639b001b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352817} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b5990a-8cb3-59df-a9d3-5b1bc0c05823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2773090-aaa0-5b0f-ab6e-ecfc1a50c98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85206" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf7089294bc72330065590a9fe54acebee6d0ab982bffb4a162407c7232898ec.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/1b/1b83e2674bba4bd6c326083f8c94496b5288de23575dc63a92b9a660eba69f67.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
